(2-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =8,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{8} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186415576 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415576 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95349" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415576 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415576 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186415576 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42bc3fa3-d1ed-5eee-8e63-dbe17bc3a3c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415576 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}8-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}8-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )((8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186415579 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415579 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "12c4c78d-1201-5f3b-8360-4897312bf437" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415579 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186415575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415580 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d5c433e-0023-555a-a2d0-920150952da3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415580 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186415578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415581 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415581 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "70681b4b-0f8d-5f23-9042-59cb06bbe55f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415581 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186415577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415582 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415582 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "944a1de7-f0ef-562b-9fc3-c518a5f0b063" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415582 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186415574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415583 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415583 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd730162-8216-5edd-a4e4-aa3f9eb30275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415583 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186415576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416445 #edb/ref 1080 "$3$ and $2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416445 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416445 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4889279a-9659-5dae-8259-b1442e0b8893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416462 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416462 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416462 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a08ff49c-3e48-5969-9cc0-4603170a0646" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416464 #edb/ref 1080 "$9$ and $2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416464 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416464 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b8b05a8d-1f8d-58ee-8ab2-e2cf7750d0f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416466 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416466 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416466 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c7d2dbc4-67b2-5124-9e27-6ec2f6fd08b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416470 #edb/ref 1080 "$9$ and $4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416470 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416470 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e93e9e61-f7d3-5cb6-85a3-a9059ed558a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186416470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416478 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416478 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416478 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186416445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416478 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bd7054d7-ed2b-5613-b2fc-90c17c4791c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416484 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416484 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416484 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416484 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186416484 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c791e385-b793-500d-9a77-3e468ca25a09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416484 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda -5\\lambda +40-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186416485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454842 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186416485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454843 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186416485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454844 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186416485 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95261" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416485 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416485 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Any $4\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n- **II.** The singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ are non-negative.\n- **III.** Any $3\\times 4$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186416485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e69dd032-aa5c-5891-a7fc-97b84703e88a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416485 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is true. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $4\\times 4$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{4\\times 3}\\times {A}_{3\\times 4}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{4\\times 4}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416486 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454848 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186416486 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454849 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186416486 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454850 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186416486 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416486 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416486 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Any $6\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n- **II.** The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ has one negative singular value.\n- **III.** Any $4\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186416486 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fef86daf-091a-52fa-bcf0-1d2b609bb348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416486 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $6\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ can't have a negative singular value.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is a $4\\times 5$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $5\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $5\\times 5$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{5\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 5}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{5\\times 5}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429230 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429230 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: linear=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 3819->991. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429230 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186048775 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186434790 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186434791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434790 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186434792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434790 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 175